{"@context":{"lean":"lean:","block":"block:","imports":{"@type":"@id"}},"@graph":[{"@id":"lean:module:QOU.Mass.LevelSelectionLogarithmic","@type":"LeanModule","name":"QOU.Mass.LevelSelectionLogarithmic","imports":["lean:module:Init","lean:module:Mathlib.Data.Real.Basic","lean:module:Mathlib.Analysis.SpecificLimits.Normed"],"declarations":9,"chunks":1},{"@id":"lean:QOU.Mass.LevelSelection.no_superlinear_selection","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.Mass.LevelSelection.no_superlinear_selection","kind":"theorem","line":152,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Mass/LevelSelectionLogarithmic.lean#L152-L198","doc":"**Super-linear (incl. bond-count) selection is excluded too.** Generalises\n`no_linear_selection`: for any level rule `kA` that is eventually at least linear\n(`A ≤ kA A` for `A ≥ A₀`), extensive binding `w (kA A) ≤ M·A` on the same tail is\nimpossible — because `c·r^A ≤ c·r^{kA A}` (monotonicity, `r > 1`) reduces it to the same\ngeometric-beats-linear contradiction. In particular the **bond-count** rule\n`kA A = A(A−1)/2` satisfies `A ≤ kA A` for `A ≥ 3`, so it is excluded (with `A₀ = 3`,\nits physical deuteron-and-up regime) — discharging the \"`A(A−1)/2` a fortiori excluded\"\nclaim that `no_linear_selection`'s docstring only asserted in prose. The threshold `A₀`\nplays the same honest role as the `A ≥ 1` restriction: it is the physical domain, not a\nboundary artifact. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.Mass.LevelSelection.no_linear_selection","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.Mass.LevelSelection.no_linear_selection","kind":"theorem","line":106,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Mass/LevelSelectionLogarithmic.lean#L106-L149","doc":"**Linear selection is genuinely impossible (exponential beats linear).** The\n`linear_selection_bound` inequality `c·r^A ≤ M·A` cannot hold for *all* `A ≥ 1`:\nsince `r > 1`, the geometric `c·r^A` eventually outgrows any linear `M·A`\n(Mathlib's `isLittleO_coe_const_pow_of_one_lt`: `(A : ℝ) =o[atTop] r^A`). So no\nextensive (linear-in-`A`) binding law is consistent with the tower lower bound —\nthe level-selection rule cannot be linear (nor bond-count `A(A−1)/2`, a fortiori).\nThis converts the file's \"linear excluded\" argument into an actual theorem.\n\nExtensivity is required only for `A ≥ 1` (the physical regime); the `A = 0` case is\ndeliberately excluded — there it would collapse to a boundary artifact\n(`w 0 ≥ c > 0` vs `M·0 = 0`) that does **not** capture the exponential-vs-linear\ncontent. The proof genuinely uses the growth lemma, not the boundary. ","formalizes":[{"@id":"block:prop:level-selection-not-linear","label":"prop:level-selection-not-linear","md":"quantum-observable-universe/mass-theory/level-selection-not-linear.md"}],"signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.Mass.LevelSelection.linear_selection_bound","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.Mass.LevelSelection.linear_selection_bound","kind":"theorem","line":95,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Mass/LevelSelectionLogarithmic.lean#L95-L103","doc":"**Linear selection is excluded.** The `kA A = A` (linear) instance of the bound:\nextensivity forces `c · r^A ≤ M · A` for every `A` — the exponential-vs-linear\ninequality that fails for large `A` when `r > 1` (`r^A` outgrows any linear `M·A`).\nSo no linear level-selection rule is consistent with extensive binding; bond-count\n`k = A(A−1)/2` is a fortiori excluded. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.Mass.LevelSelection.level_geometric_bound","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.Mass.LevelSelection.level_geometric_bound","kind":"theorem","line":78,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Mass/LevelSelectionLogarithmic.lean#L78-L93","doc":"**Conditional theorem — the level's exponential is at most linear in `A`.**\nGiven the tower lower bound (`TowerGeometricLowerBound w r c`) and *extensive*\nbinding `w (kA A) ≤ M · A` at the selected level `kA A`, we get\n\n  `c · r^(kA A) ≤ M · A`.\n\nDividing by `c > 0` gives `r^(kA A) ≤ (M/c)·A`; since `r > 1`, taking `log_r`\n(monotone) then gives `kA A ≤ log_r((M/c)·A)` — the level grows **at most\nlogarithmically** in `A`. A linear rule `kA A = A` would force `c · r^A ≤ M · A`\n(exponential ≤ linear), impossible for large `A`; bond-count `A(A−1)/2` is worse.\nBoth are excluded — matching the scale-free probe (`k∝A` R²=0.67, `k∝`bonds R²=0.49\nvs log-type R²≈0.88). Proved outright from the class hypothesis. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.Mass.LevelSelection.TowerGeometricLowerBound.lower_bound","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.Mass.LevelSelection.TowerGeometricLowerBound.lower_bound","kind":"theorem","line":76,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Mass/LevelSelectionLogarithmic.lean#L76-L76","doc":"The lower bound: `c · r^k ≤ w k` for every jet level `k ≥ k₀`. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.Mass.LevelSelection.TowerGeometricLowerBound.c_pos","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.Mass.LevelSelection.TowerGeometricLowerBound.c_pos","kind":"theorem","line":74,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Mass/LevelSelectionLogarithmic.lean#L74-L74","doc":"`c > 0` — positive leading constant. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.Mass.LevelSelection.TowerGeometricLowerBound.one_lt_r","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.Mass.LevelSelection.TowerGeometricLowerBound.one_lt_r","kind":"theorem","line":72,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Mass/LevelSelectionLogarithmic.lean#L72-L72","doc":"`r > 1` — the bound is genuinely growing (the verified growth is\nsuper-exponential, with `r` effectively unbounded; any fixed `r > 1` is a\nvalid — weaker — lower-bound base). ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.Mass.LevelSelection.TowerGeometricLowerBound.mk","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.Mass.LevelSelection.TowerGeometricLowerBound.mk","kind":"ctor","line":68,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Mass/LevelSelectionLogarithmic.lean#L68-L68","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.Mass.LevelSelection.TowerGeometricLowerBound","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.Mass.LevelSelection.TowerGeometricLowerBound","kind":"class","line":48,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Mass/LevelSelectionLogarithmic.lean#L48-L76","doc":"**The tower growth lower bound, above a threshold.** A geometric lower bound\n`c · r^k ≤ w k` on the branched tower weight `w = w_std`, holding for `k ≥ k₀`, with\nbase `r`, constant `c` and threshold `k₀` carried as **parameters**.\n\n**Why the threshold is not cosmetic.** This class previously demanded the bound for\n*every* `k`, and in that form it is **false** at the real `w_std`: at `k = 0` it reads\n`c ≤ w_std 0 = 0`, against `c_pos`. `QOU.Mass.WStd.not_towerGeometricLowerBound_wStd`\nproves that refutation for every `r` and `c`, so no choice of constants repaired it —\nthe threshold is the fix. The vanishing is structural: no diagram of fewer than three\ncells contains `(2,1)`, and `fDimSkew` guards on containment.\n\n**Its intended instance is `k₀ = 3`** — exactly where `w_std` stops vanishing, so the\nclass covers the entire range on which any lower bound is possible. See\n`QOU.Mass.WStd.towerGeometricLowerBound_wStd`.\n\nThe *measured* growth is strictly stronger than geometric — successive ratios strictly\nincreasing, `1.38 → 2.88` for `k ≤ 10` (`wlambda_wstd_growth_bound_probe.py`), and the\nproved square bound `k!/(81·2^k) ≤ w_std(k)²` is genuinely super-exponential. The\ngeometric form here is the weaker shadow of that, and is all the logarithmic conclusion\nbelow needs. ","signatureChunk":0}]}